# Getting Started From Zero
## A No-Assumptions Guide to Building This Project

This assumes you know nothing yet — not SUMO, not reinforcement learning, not Docker, not even how to run a command. Every step says **what to do**, **what it means**, and **what you should see** when it works. Follow it in order; don't skip ahead.

---

## Part 0: Understand What You're Actually Building (5 min read)

Before touching a keyboard, get this mental picture straight — it will make every later step make sense instead of feeling like magic commands.

**The problem:** A traffic light at a real intersection usually just cycles on a timer (30 seconds green, 3 seconds yellow, repeat) no matter what's actually happening on the road. That's dumb when one direction is empty and another is backed up.

**The idea:** Instead of a timer, we want a "smart" controller that looks at the actual traffic and decides which direction should get green right now. We teach this controller using **Reinforcement Learning (RL)** — a way of training software by letting it try things, see the result, and get better over time, similar to how you'd learn a video game by playing it repeatedly rather than reading a manual.

**The pieces:**
- **SUMO** — a free traffic simulator. It's a fake city intersection running on your computer, with fake cars driving through it. Nothing here is real traffic; it's entirely simulated, which means we can experiment safely.
- **TraCI** — the "remote control" that lets a Python program talk to SUMO while it's running: read how many cars are waiting, and tell the traffic light which color to show.
- **DQN (Deep Q-Network)** — the specific RL technique/algorithm we use to train the "brain" that decides which light to turn green. "Deep" just means it uses a neural network (a small one, in our case) instead of a lookup table.
- **Docker / Codespaces** — a way to get a ready-made computer environment (with SUMO etc. already installed) instead of installing everything manually on your own laptop, which is fragile and error-prone.

**What "done" looks like:** A trained AI controller that, when tested in the simulator, makes cars wait less on average than a normal timer-based traffic light — with numbers/graphs to prove it.

That's the whole project. Everything below is just "how do I actually build that, one small piece at a time."

